ttacks against  journalists in that has intensified with  a shutdown of the largest broadcaster  just last month I suppose I walked in  feeling and knowing that there could be  a worst-case scenario that would is even  worse than this one and so when I  listened to it I just I tried hard not  to get angry and then to figure out how  do we continue doing our jobs better  given these attacks are you prepared to  go to jail there you know I think this  is a question I had to confront a while  back because Amal Clooney actually just  pointed out to me that the cumulative  charges the maximum jailed penalty for  all of them is almost a hundred years  and so I think the question is that not  challenging these cases would mean that  I would give up the rights that are  guaranteed in the Constitution not just  freedom of the press but also the rights  of Filipinos to information to hold  power to count now you say this verdict  that's been handed down today as part of  a campaign against you and your your  website rattler calm by president duty  and his government what evidence do you  have for such a campaign actions of the  government part of the reason rattler  came into the crosshairs is because we  continue challenging the number of  deaths in a very brutal drug war the  Commission on Human Rights in the  Philippines estimates months ago that it  was over 27,000 people killed impunity  there and then here you can see that you  know we were under trial today yes the  verdict came out today but the justice  system was also under trial because  what's happened in the last few years is  the weaponization of the law the law  tweaked legal acrobatics used against  journalists perceived critics  opposition's and in that sense that also  follows the kind of  exponential attacks what we called the  propaganda machine on social media so  the narratives of the attacks come out  first on social media and then the law  the weaponization of the law follows  come on eight cases that 